well I think the first thing to say is  the financial crisis was a really big  and very painful lesson for all of us I  think it's not just the money that had  to be put in taxpayers money to the  banks but also the crisis and the  recession that followed on average  probably meant that everyone in the UK  was about 20,000 pounds worse off than  they would have been so it's really  important that we've learned the lessons  and the system is safer I do think it's  safer I think it's simpler and I think  it's stronger than it was before the  crisis however there are three reasons  for that the reforms we put in have  tried to do a number of things first  we've stopped banks taking some of the  risky activities they were taking and  equally important made sure that they're  measuring properly they're scoring  properly the real risk they're taking  and managing that we do that through the  rules but also through a much more  intrusive supervision of the banks and  their operations and and next I think  banks have to take risks and those risks  will sometimes go wrong  banks will make losses but you have to  make sure that when they take those  losses they can absorb them without just  falling over and that's where Bank  capital is important the bank capital in  the system now is about ten times as  much as it was before the crisis and  it's not just the quantity of capital  it's the quality it has to be real money  basically shareholders funds that can  absorb losses when loans and other  things go wrong and we're sure the bank  can keep operating and keep providing  the sorts of services lending  transaction accounts that we all depend  on and finally it's not just capital  it's also liquidity because sometimes  the market dries out banks can no longer  borrow in the market and they have to be  able to withstand those periods of  market stress and carry on providing  services and banks in the UK and now  having about four times as much  liquidity about six hundred billion  pounds compared to what they had before  the crisis and the last thing I say is  banks will you know the bank may well  fail and then you have to ensure that if  a bank fails it can do so safely without  just cutting off the services it  provides  economy and it was because banks  couldn't fail safely without cutting off  those services that the taxpayer had to  step in during the crisis to save the  economy from the banks and we're putting  in place a regime that can ensure that  if a bank fails it can be wound down  safely and the losses can be borne by  the people who profit from the banking  good times and not my tax payer so for  those reasons I think the bank is the  banking system is safer and stronger  than it was you've mentioned that banks  aren't engaging in bad practices anymore  but yet we are seeing for example an  increase in things like interest free  credit periods being offered by credit  card lenders Vaman do you worry that  stresses are building up again in the  system it's one of the key reforms that  we made after the system was to give the  Bank of England the responsibility for  the stability of the financial systems a  whole its my direct responsibilities  deputy governor for financial stability  to ensure that we can scan and monitor  problems risk that might be building up  in the system exactly the sort of thing  that you mentioned and the financial  policy committee of the Bank of England  does that it does that four times a year  we publish our reports on risk twice  here we've taken action for example on  mortgages on bank capital before those  risk build-up to greatly another moment  we're looking very closely at this  question of consumer lending lending to  consumers some of the credit card  practices we've seen to see whether they  are a risk to the financial system and  whether action needs to be taken and  we'll be reporting on that at our next  meeting now you're older brussels hand  how much of a challenge is brexit going 